# Build context for the three-stage image. The image is built from source, so
# the context only needs Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock/build.rs/src plus the dashboard's
# own sources; everything below is either rebuilt inside the build or has no
# business in an image layer.
#
# Note the two exclusions that matter most:
#   * dashboard/dist and dashboard/node_modules — the dashboard stage builds
#     these itself; copying them in would bake a stale bundle from the developer's
#     working tree into an artifact that is supposed to be reproducible from the
#     commit.
#   * .git — the revision is passed as the GIT_COMMIT build argument instead, so
#     the context stays small and the image metadata is explicit rather than
#     inferred from whatever the checkout happened to contain.

# --- VCS -------------------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.github

# --- Rust build tree -------------------------------------------------------
# `benches/` is *not* excluded: Cargo.toml declares a [[bench]] target, so the
# manifest cannot even be parsed without the file present.
target
**/*.rs.bk

# --- Dashboard build tree --------------------------------------------------
dashboard/dist
dashboard/node_modules
dashboard/.vite
**/node_modules
**/.pnpm-store

# --- Local state: never ship a database or a log into an image -------------
# A copy of the ledger inside the image would be a second, forkable source of
# truth for usage accounting.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
deploy/data
logs
*.log

# --- Local configuration and secrets --------------------------------------
# The image takes its configuration from a mounted file. A config or a key
# committed into a layer is a credential that cannot be revoked by rotating the
# config, only by rebuilding every image that ever copied it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
config.yaml
config.local.yaml
deploy/config/partner-portal.local.yaml

# --- Deployment and CI (referenced by the operator, not by the build) ------
deploy
# The local dev fixtures, for the same reason: a developer runs them, the image
# does not. `dev/partner-portal.dev.yaml` carries plaintext dev credentials, and
# a config in a layer is a credential that survives deleting the file.
dev
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.*.yml
deny.toml
CODEOWNERS

# --- Docs, licence and editor noise (LICENSE is copied into release tarballs
# by the release workflow from the git checkout, not from the image context) --
docs
*.md
!README.md
.idea
.vscode
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Test scratch ----------------------------------------------------------
coverage
test-output
tmp
